## Decisions and Constraints

- Task types are `paper:metadata_extract` and `paper:catalog_resolve`.
- Asynq payloads contain identifiers and an optional `markdown_sha256`; never
  Markdown, PDF bytes, Blocks, credentials, or external provider responses.
- Extraction workers always reload durable Markdown and reject a changed
  SHA-256. Catalog workers validate the SHA-256 only when the query was tied to
  a parsed revision; metadata-only and source-free lookup omit it.
- Python produces candidates and evidence only; it does not query external
  catalogs or write the database.
- Go owns catalog access, candidate scoring, automatic persistence, and
  retries. Users correct metadata only after the automatic pipeline finishes
  or cannot produce a satisfactory result.
- The default catalog order starts with arXiv. Operator-configured order is
  preserved without query-dependent reordering. Within the arXiv adapter, an
  arXiv URL, `arXiv:` identifier, or `10.48550/arXiv.*` DOI uses exact
  `id_list` lookup instead of broad title/DOI search.
- Exact DOI and arXiv identifiers remain priority matches. A differing DOI is
  not negative evidence because providers may expose different identifiers;
  non-exact candidates are ranked primarily by title similarity.
- Local paper reconciliation tries normalized DOI first, then strict normalized
  title. Title fallback excludes self and unresolved/deleting documents and
  rejects conflicting known authors or publication years.
- `paper_metadata.metadata_source` records the current snapshot origin.
  Atomic persistence precedence is `manual > catalog > extraction > unknown`;
  an asynchronous lower-priority writer may not replace a higher-priority
  snapshot.
- Catalog lookup is independent of document parsing status. Uploaded, queued,
  processing, ready, failed, cancelled, and unknown documents may resolve from
  durable metadata. Deleting/missing documents are rejected. A supplied
  Markdown SHA-256 is checked, but readiness is not.
- Superseded 2026-07-28: catalog enqueue was identity-driven and could begin
  from a durable DOI before extraction completed.
- Catalog resolution is chained by the extraction worker only after extraction
  succeeds and persists its result. A usable DOI or extracted title is then
  sufficient to enqueue catalog resolution.
- Paper extraction tasks use the default queue with a 2-minute/3-retry default;
  catalog tasks use the low queue with a 10-minute/5-retry default. These
  policies are configurable through `PAPER_*_TASK_TIMEOUT` and
  `PAPER_*_TASK_MAX_RETRY`.
- Superseded 2026-07-28: recognition, paper extraction, and paper catalog
  enqueue methods shared one typed `DocumentTaskEnqueuer`.
- Queue dispatch uses one generic, context-aware `TaskEnqueuer` implemented by
  `*asynq.Client`; task constructors own payload validation, serialization,
  queue, retry, timeout, and deduplication policy.
- Paper-info stages rely on Asynq retry/dead-letter behavior and explicit
  retries; they do not run a separate database-scanning housekeeping service.
- Stale/cancelled paper tasks are revoked without dead-letter creation.
  Invalid or deterministic unsupported work is archived immediately; transient
  gRPC/provider/429/5xx failures retry and honor provider Retry-After.
- Real-provider tests are opt-in through `PAPER_CATALOG_LIVE_TEST=1`; normal
  tests remain offline and deterministic. Credentials come only from the
  existing environment variables.
- `DocumentService` owns source-file lifecycle, parsing state, Markdown, and
  source-map Blocks; it must not grow into the paper-domain service.
- A dedicated `PaperService` owns canonical `paper_metadata` operations and
  `paper_citations` operations, including unresolved placeholders. Its
  constructor injects
  `DocumentRepository`, `PaperMetadataRepository`, and
  `PaperCitationRepository` directly rather than reaching through
  `DocumentService.GetRepository()`.
- `PaperInfoService` remains separate from `PaperService`: paper-info processing
  coordinates Python extraction and external catalogs, then uses the paper
  domain boundary to persist canonical metadata and citation edges.
- Raw references remain `[]string` during extraction.
- A reference that cannot yet be matched to a stored paper uses an internal
  placeholder `Document` with status `unknown`; the citation row also retains
  the exact extraction reference in `raw_reference`. Placeholder documents
  have no readable source file and must never enter document recognition.
- Catalog lookup may target an `unknown` placeholder before its source file is
  uploaded. It builds the query from `paper_metadata` first, then fills missing
  DOI, source URL, or title from incoming `raw_reference` values. A local match
  repoints incoming citations; otherwise the placeholder receives canonical
  catalog metadata and remains source-free.
- `paper_metadata` is both the current extraction snapshot and the canonical
  catalog result. Extraction writes title/authors/abstract directly; catalog
  resolution reads those fields and may replace them with the selected work.
- Raw extracted references are persisted directly in `paper_citations`; no
  paper-info run or catalog-candidate archive is maintained.
- Superseded 2026-07-28: do not persist extraction runs, per-field provenance,
  catalog candidate history, or separate extraction/lookup status. Asynq
  retries/dead letters handle operational failures, and users correct the
  final metadata when automation is insufficient.
- Superseded 2026-07-27: catalog results were originally designed with
  `resolved`, `no_match`, `ambiguous`, `failed`, and `stale` states.
- Extraction and catalog result types now record only terminal `success` or
  `failed`. Queue lifecycle remains in Asynq; detailed no-match, ambiguity,
  stale-work, and cancellation reasons belong in task errors or result
  metadata rather than these database-facing status enums.
- Generated protobuf files must be regenerated, never edited manually.

